        "id": 1002022
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-08-21T21:16:18Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "why?  if it only requests 1.5A, can\u0027t we treat it the same as non-pd devices for the purposes of distributing power?\n\nsimilarly, if it requests 3A for a while, then re-requests only 1.5A since it doesn\u0027t need it, it would be great to reallocate the power to the 4th device (the tablet).\n\nwe don\u0027t *have* to give 3A back to the non-PD devices, but it would be good to at least redistribute amongst PD-capable devices.\n\nI suspect we\u0027ll ultimately have two bits (PD/non-PD, \u003e1.5A/1.5A) per port along with the order of connection going into this policy algorithm, and a simple priority selection that picks who gets 3A, run each time a state changes.  So the above behavior would just naturally come through.\n\nWe can complicate things if we consider that PD sources and PD devices can advertise and request a range of currents (not just 1.5A/3A), but there\u0027s diminishing returns there...",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 141,
        "startChar": 12,
        "endLine": 142,
        "endChar": 16
      },
      "revId": "74981f1f20c27cee1fb9ff1051399757c7330420",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "73086c41_c0b97a76",
        "filename": "docs/usb_power.md",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 142,
      "author": {
        "id": 1259838
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-08-21T22:44:08Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I agree that it is technically possible to be even more dynamic (and efficient) with our power allocation, but I am concerned about the user\u0027s mental model/perception of what is happening.\n\nI am trying to keep things as predictable as possible. I think users will be able to grasp that when I plug in my phone it gets fast charge. If I plug in another device, it does not get fast charged until I remove the first device. I think switching where the extra 1.5A of current is going based on dynamic power contracts will potentially confuse the end-user since there was not  any interaction from the user to catalyze a change in power distribution.\n\nIf were we making some fancy charging station, I would be all in on distributing the power as efficiently as possible (because I think that would also align with user expectations), but for a Chromebook, I think that the predictable correlation of insertion order and power distribution  is more important.\n\n----\n\nI also think that the likelihood of a device giving up its 3A contract is very low too and very device specific (again leading to potential customer confusion)",
      "parentUuid": "9c097dd7_41e16941",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 141,
        "startChar": 12,
        "endLine": 142,
        "endChar": 16
      },
      "revId": "74981f1f20c27cee1fb9ff1051399757c7330420",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "af1b5296_38a3279b",
        "filename": "docs/usb_power.md",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 142,
      "author": {
        "id": 1002022
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-08-21T22:53:45Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "OK, but what about the case where you have a PD device that never requests more than 1.5A?  Why does such a PD device pull power away from a non-PD 3A device, but a PD device that actually wants 3A can\u0027t pull it away from the 1.5A PD device?\n\nThe assumption here (assuming reasonably simple PD implementations) is that a PD device that requests \u003c\u003d1.5A could be a dock, or a USB4 flash drive, or something else that doesn\u0027t charge.",
      "parentUuid": "73086c41_c0b97a76",
